<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<pretext>
    <article xml:id="worksheet">

<worksheet xml:id="wkst-mcmc-4x4">
  <title>An MCMC Exercise on a 4×4 Grid</title>

  <p>
    In this activity we model a “state” as a <m>4\times 4</m> grid of equal-population precincts.
    A districting plan assigns each cell to one of two districts, <m>A</m> or <m>B</m>.
    We require:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><p><em>Balance:</em> each district has exactly 8 cells.</p></li>
    <li><p><em>Contiguity:</em> within each district, every cell can be reached from every other by a path of edge-adjacent (not diagonal) cells that stays inside the district.</p></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Think of each valid plan as a state in a Markov chain.
    We will define a simple MCMC rule that proposes a local “swap” along the boundary between the districts.
  </p>


    <p>
      Below are three valid plans.  In each grid, the symbols indicate which district each cell belongs to.
    </p>

    <p><em>Plan</em> <m>P_0</m> (vertical split):</p>
    <md><mrow>
      P_0=
      \begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      \end{array}
    </mrow></md>

    <p><em>Plan</em> <m>P_1</m> (a “staircase” boundary):</p>
    <md><mrow>
      P_1=
      \begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      A \amp B \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      A \amp A \amp A \amp B\\ \hline
      \end{array}
    </mrow></md>

    <p><em>Plan</em> <m>P_2</m> (the mirror staircase):</p>
    <md><mrow>
      P_2 =
      \begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      A \amp A \amp A \amp B\\ \hline
      A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      A \amp B \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
      \end{array}
    </mrow></md>

    <p>
      (Each plan has 8 <m>A</m>-cells and 8 <m>B</m>-cells, and you can check contiguity by tracing edge-adjacent paths.)
    </p>

    <p>
      A single MCMC step will propose a new plan by attempting a <em>boundary swap</em>.
    </p>

    <p><em>Boundary-swap proposal rule.</em></p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Choose uniformly at random an <em>edge</em> (shared side) between two adjacent cells.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If the two cells are in different districts (one labeled <m>A</m>, one labeled <m>B</m>), propose swapping their labels.
          (This preserves the 8–8 balance automatically.)
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Accept the swap <em>only if</em> both districts remain contiguous after the swap.
          Otherwise, reject the proposal and stay at the current plan.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      This produces a Markov chain on the space of valid plans.
      If the proposal mechanism is symmetric (choosing an edge uniformly),
      then this “accept-if-valid, otherwise-stay” rule targets the uniform distribution on valid plans
      (informally: you do not prefer any valid plan over another; you are just exploring the space).
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-grid-contiguity">
      <title>Contiguity Check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of the plans <m>P_0, P_1, P_2</m> above:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Verify that district <m>A</m> is contiguous.</p></li>
          <li><p>Verify that district <m>B</m> is contiguous.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Give a short explanation (for example, describe a spanning “path of cells” that connects the district).
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-grid-one-swap">
      <title>One Proposed Swap from <m>P_1</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In plan <m>P_1</m>, focus on the adjacent pair of cells in row 2, columns 2 and 3 (the pair <m>(2,2)</m> and <m>(2,3)</m>).
          (Row numbers increase downward; column numbers increase to the right.)
        </p>
        <p>
          These two cells have labels <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, so the boundary-swap rule proposes swapping them.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Write down the new plan <m>P'</m> obtained by swapping those two labels.</p></li>
          <li><p>Does district <m>A</m> remain contiguous in <m>P'</m>? Explain.</p></li>
          <li><p>Does district <m>B</m> remain contiguous in <m>P'</m>? Explain.</p></li>
          <li><p>Is the swap accepted or rejected?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Students’ answers will depend on their contiguity check; the key is that balance is preserved automatically,
          but contiguity may fail if the swapped cell was a “bridge” connecting part of a district.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-grid-list-legal">
      <title>List the Accepted Swaps from <m>P_1</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider all edges (shared sides) in the <m>4\times 4</m> grid.
          For plan <m>P_1</m>, an edge is <em>eligible</em> if its two cells have different labels.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>How many eligible edges does <m>P_1</m> have?</p></li>
          <li><p>For each eligible edge, decide whether swapping across that edge would be accepted (both districts remain contiguous) or rejected.</p></li>
          <li><p>How many accepted swaps does <m>P_1</m> have?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-grid-transition-prob">
      <title>A Transition Probability</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose the chain is currently at <m>P_1</m> and we choose uniformly among all grid edges.
          (There are 24 edges total in a <m>4\times 4</m> grid: 12 horizontal and 12 vertical.)
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Using your work from the previous problem, compute the probability that the chain stays at <m>P_1</m> in one step.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute the probability that the chain moves to each distinct new plan reachable from <m>P_1</m> in one accepted swap.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          (If multiple edges produce the same resulting plan, combine their probabilities.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          If <m>a</m> edges lead to accepted swaps and the remaining edges either are not eligible or are rejected,
          then <m>\Pr(\text{move})=a/24</m> and <m>\Pr(\text{stay})=1-a/24</m>.
          Probabilities of specific moves depend on how many edges produce each resulting plan.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-grid-statistic">
      <title>Connecting to Gerrymandering: An Observable</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Define a simple statistic <m>f(P)</m> on plans as follows:
          in each district, Party A wins the district if Party A has a majority of votes in that district.
        </p>

        <p>
          To keep this toy model concrete, assign each cell a fixed vote label (Party A or Party B) using the pattern below:
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          \begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}
          \hline
          A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
          A \amp A \amp B \amp B\\ \hline
          B \amp B \amp A \amp A\\ \hline
          B \amp B \amp A \amp A\\ \hline
          \end{array}
        </mrow></md>

        <p>
          For each plan <m>P_0, P_1, P_2</m>, compute <m>f(P)</m>, the number of districts won by Party A.
        </p>

        <p>
          Explain (in one or two sentences) what it would mean, in this setting, for an enacted plan to be an outlier in the MCMC ensemble.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-grid-linear-algebra">
  <title>Linear Algebra Structure of the Markov Chain</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      In this exercise we study the boundary-swap Markov chain on the space of valid
      districting plans for the <m>4\times 4</m> grid with two contiguous districts of size 8.
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose we focus on three particular plans introduced earlier:
    </p>

    <md><mrow>
      \Omega'=\{P_0,P_1,P_2\}.
    </mrow></md>

    <p>
      We will analyze how the Markov chain moves among these plans.
    </p>

    <p>
      Assume the chain proposes swaps by choosing uniformly among the 24 grid edges.
      From prior work, suppose the accepted swaps are:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><p>From <m>P_0</m>, exactly 4 edges lead to <m>P_1</m> and 4 edges lead to <m>P_2</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>From <m>P_1</m>, 3 edges lead to <m>P_0</m>, 2 lead to <m>P_2</m>, and the rest are rejected.</p></li>
      <li><p>From <m>P_2</m>, 3 edges lead to <m>P_0</m>, 2 lead to <m>P_1</m>, and the rest are rejected.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      (Rejected swaps mean the chain stays at the current plan.)
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Compute the one-step transition probabilities and write the transition matrix <m>T</m>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Let <m>e_0,e_1,e_2</m> be the standard basis vectors corresponding to starting entirely in plans
          <m>P_0,P_1,P_2</m>.
          Interpret <m>T e_0</m> probabilistically.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          If the initial distribution is
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          \mathbf{v}^{(0)}=
          \begin{pmatrix}
          1\\0\\0
          \end{pmatrix},
        </mrow></md>

        <p>
          compute <m>\mathbf{v}^{(1)} = T\mathbf{v}^{(0)}</m>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Explain in words what the vector <m>\mathbf{v}^{(2)} = T^2\mathbf{v}^{(0)}</m> represents.
          (You do not need to compute it explicitly.)
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Why must 1 be an eigenvalue of <m>T</m>?
          (Hint: use the fact that rows sum to 1.)
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          What does the corresponding eigenvector represent in the context of districting plans?
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Each move probability is the number of accepted edges divided by 24.
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          T=
          \begin{pmatrix}
          16/24 \amp 4/24 \amp 4/24\\
          3/24 \amp 19/24 \amp 2/24\\
          3/24 \amp 2/24 \amp 19/24
          \end{pmatrix}.
        </mrow></md>

        <p>
          The diagonal entries reflect rejected proposals.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The vector <m>e_0=(1,0,0)^T</m> represents certainty of starting in plan <m>P_0</m>.
          Then
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          Te_0=
          \begin{pmatrix}
          16/24\\
          3/24\\
          3/24
          \end{pmatrix},
        </mrow></md>

        <p>
          which gives the probability distribution after one step.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          This is the same computation:
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          \mathbf{v}^{(1)}=
          \begin{pmatrix}
          16/24\\
          3/24\\
          3/24
          \end{pmatrix}.
        </mrow></md>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The vector <m>T^2\mathbf{v}^{(0)}</m> gives the probability distribution over plans after two MCMC steps.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Since rows sum to 1, the vector of all ones is a left eigenvector.
          Therefore 1 is an eigenvalue of <m>T</m>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The corresponding right eigenvector (normalized to sum to 1)
          is the stationary distribution of districting plans —
          the long-run sampling frequency of each plan.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </solution>

</exercise>

    <remark>
      <p>
        This activity emphasizes what MCMC is doing in redistricting: it samples from a huge discrete space of plans under constraints,
        then computes an “observable” (seat count, efficiency gap, etc.) as a function <m>f(P)</m>.
        The Monte Carlo average approximates the discrete integral <m>\sum_{P\in \Omega} f(P)\pi(P)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The boundary-swap move is intentionally local and easy to implement by hand.
        It also naturally illustrates why contiguity constraints make the space complicated:
        some swaps break connectivity and must be rejected.
      </p>
    </remark>
    </worksheet>
    </article>
    </pretext>